<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">League of Prizren</h1><p class="st-meta">1878 CE – 1881 CE · capital Prizren</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Albanian political and military league formed in 1878 to defend Albanian-inhabited lands from partition, and the starting point of the Albanian national movement.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-stefano">San Stefano</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1878 assigned Albanian-inhabited districts to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. On 10 June 1878 Albanian notables met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> and formed a league to resist the partition, at first with tacit Ottoman approval, under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ymer-prizreni">Ymer Prizreni</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdyl-frasheri">Abdyl Frashëri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulejman-vokshi">Sulejman Vokshi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The league raised armed volunteers and set up local committees, mainly among Muslim landowners and highland clans, though Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> also took part. It fought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plav">Plav</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusinje">Gusinje</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdyl-frasheri">Abdyl Frashëri</a> it moved from defending Ottoman territory to demanding a single autonomous Albanian province, and in 1880 it took over administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Alarmed by the league's autonomy demands and under European pressure, the Ottoman government sent Dervish Pasha's army, which crushed it in 1881. Its leaders were imprisoned or exiled, but its programme inspired Albanian nationalists until independence in 1912.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/league-of-prizren.jpg" alt="League of Prizren" width="320" height="287"><figcaption>Image: GalaxMaps · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:League_of_Prizren_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1878 CE – 1881 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Prizren</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abdyl Frashëri, Ymer Prizreni, Sulejman Vokshi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Albanian League of Prizren</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1880&amp;focus=league-of-prizren" aria-label="League of Prizren on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5183 675 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where League of Prizren lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5503 931L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5503 931h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5503 931h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Prizren. <a href="/atlas?year=1880&amp;focus=league-of-prizren">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

